  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 140 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nSunday through Wednesday:\n\nSynopsis: Above normal temperatures will continue to prevail during \nthe day Sunday as a slow-moving cold front drifts into northern \nWisconsin. With frontal forcing remaining well-removed from the \nregion, dry conditions are currently expected along with the warm \ntemperatures. Moving into next week, global ensemble guidance is in \ngeneral agreement for 1) an upper shortwave passage during the \nMonday-Tuesday timeframe and 2) the arrival of a larger-scale upper \ntrough and surface frontal push by the middle of the week/conclusion \nof the long term period. Northerly surface mass responses affiliated \nwith the early week shortwave will help shove the Sunday cold front \nthrough southern Wisconsin on Monday, bringing more seasonable \ntemperatures to the area through the first half of next week. Rain \nchances return to the forecast during the passage of both \nperturbations, with Monday night into Tuesday & Wednesday night into \nThursday currently favored for the most widespread activity. \nThunderstorm chances appear low at this time. \n\nMonday through Wednesday: Have largely maintained an NBM forecast \nthrough this portion of the period given ensemble agreement \nregarding synoptic pattern evolution. Said forecast pulls high \ntemperatures back into the mid-50s to low-60s, in addition to \nbringing chance (~25-40%) precip probabilities to the region Monday \nnight/Tuesday and Wednesday night. Barring changes in consistency \nacross the global ensemble suite, would anticipate general stability \nin this forecast into this weekend. Will continue to monitor for any \npossible changes over coming updates. \n\nQuigley",
